WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:07.349
At night in Dunsinane castle, a doctor and one of Lady Macbeth's serving women watched outside her bedroom.

2
00:00:07.349 --> 00:00:12.531
"How many nights has she been sleepwalking?" asked the doctor quietly.

3
00:00:12.532 --> 00:00:18.753
"Since the king and queen moved to the royal palace here at Dunsinane," explained the serving woman.

4
00:00:18.754 --> 00:00:27.213
"She rises from her bed, deep in sleep, and wanders the castle repeating words to herself—shhh, here she comes."

5
00:00:27.214 --> 00:00:30.467
Lady Macbeth stepped slowly out of the bedroom.

6
00:00:30.468 --> 00:00:36.777
Her pale nightgown was ghostly in the moonlight, and her hair hung loose and disheveled on her shoulders.

7
00:00:36.778 --> 00:00:41.063
She kept rubbing her hands fretfully, as if she were washing them.

8
00:00:41.064 --> 00:00:43.409
"Here's another spot," she mumbled.

9
00:00:43.409 --> 00:00:51.031
Then more loudly, "Out, damned spot! Out! Who would have thought the old king had so much blood in him?"

10
00:00:51.032 --> 00:00:55.567
"Did you hear that?" whispered the serving woman as they followed.

11
00:00:55.568 --> 00:01:01.392
"Banquo's buried; how can he come out of his grave?" continued Lady Macbeth.

12
00:01:01.393 --> 00:01:08.455
"The thane of Fife had a wife; where is she now? Oh, will these hands never be clean?"

13
00:01:08.456 --> 00:01:11.495
She stopped and lifted a hand to her face.

14
00:01:11.496 --> 00:01:18.178
"There's the smell of blood on them. All the perfumes of Arabia will not sweeten this hand.

15
00:01:18.179 --> 00:01:24.884
Lady Macbeth wandered back to her bedroom, still rubbing her hands in her sleep. The doctor shook his head.

16
00:01:24.885 --> 00:01:29.852
"She needs the help of heaven more than she needs a physician," he said.

17
00:01:29.853 --> 00:01:32.638
"This disease is beyond cure."

18
00:01:32.639 --> 00:01:38.281
In the countryside in the early morning, a band of Scottish soldiers marched near Dunsinane.

19
00:01:38.282 --> 00:01:46.574
Word had spread that Malcolm and Macduff had arrived in Scotland with an army, and many who had suffered under Macbeth had decided to take up arms.

20
00:01:46.575 --> 00:01:55.275
A mile ahead, the soldiers could see the flags and tents of a great army camped beside a forest, and eagerly, they marched forward to join it.

21
00:01:55.276 --> 00:02:00.796
Inside his castle, Macbeth was pacing back and forth and raging at his servants.

22
00:02:00.797 --> 00:02:10.077
"Bring me no more reports! I don't care how big their army is! Until Birnam Wood comes to Dunsinane, I'll never shake with fear.

23
00:02:10.078 --> 00:02:15.387
Who's that boy Malcolm? Was he not born of a woman? He cannot harm me!"

24
00:02:15.388 --> 00:02:21.269
The servants left him, and Macbeth sat down. "I am sick at heart," he mumbled.

25
00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:28.176
"I have lived long enough. I can no longer hope for honor, love, or friends?only curses."

26
00:02:28.177 --> 00:02:37.684
Then, suddenly infuriated, he yelled, "Servants! Servants! Bring me my armor! I'll fight until my flesh is hacked from my bones!"

27
00:02:37.685 --> 00:02:42.525
Malcolm, Macduff, Siward, and Ross, meanwhile, were discussing the coming battle.

28
00:02:42.526 --> 00:02:47.354
They stood at the front of their camp, looking up toward the castle on Dunsinane Hill.

29
00:02:47.355 --> 00:02:52.629
"Macbeth has not deployed his army to the field," Siward was telling the others.

30
00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:57.422
"All reports say that he is staying defended inside Dunsinane Castle."

31
00:02:57.424 --> 00:03:01.086
"This gives us the advantage," said Macduff.

32
00:03:01.086 --> 00:03:04.469
"What wood is this by the camp?" asked Malcolm.

33
00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.877
"Birnam Wood," said Ross.

34
00:03:06.877 --> 00:03:11.924
"Tell every soldier to cut down a branch and carry it before him," Malcolm said.

35
00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:17.971
"This will disguise our numbers as we approach." The others agreed and sent word to their soldiers.

36
00:03:17.972 --> 00:03:21.931
While Macbeth was putting on his armor, a servant came rushing in.

37
00:03:21.932 --> 00:03:27.844
"The queen, my lord, is dead!" cried the servant. "She has taken her own life!"

38
00:03:27.845 --> 00:03:35.836
Macbeth turned to him distractedly. "She should have died after all this was over. Then there would have been time for proper words."

39
00:03:35.837 --> 00:03:39.757
He looked down at the pieces of his armor still waiting to be put on.

40
00:03:39.758 --> 00:03:44.408
Suddenly, all his ambition and wicked deeds seemed pointless and empty.

41
00:03:44.409 --> 00:03:48.582
"Tomorrow, and tomorrow, and tomorrow," he sighed.

42
00:03:48.583 --> 00:03:59.011
"Life creeps in this sluggish way from day to day to the last syllable of recorded time. Our yesterdays just light the path to dusty death."

43
00:03:59.012 --> 00:04:03.265
He reached out to a candle and snuffed out the flame between his fingers.

44
00:04:03.267 --> 00:04:11.282
"Out, out, brief candle! Life is nothing but a poor actor who struts his hour upon the stage.

45
00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:15.878
It's a tale full of sound and fury, signifying nothing."

46
00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:23.768
Macbeth's thoughts were interrupted by another messenger. "My lord, I don't know how to report what I saw," he began.

47
00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:27.301
"Well, use your tongue," snapped Macbeth.

48
00:04:27.303 --> 00:04:36.191
"As I stood watch in the tower, I looked toward Birnam Wood, and . . ." he hesitated. "I thought I saw the wood begin to move."

49
00:04:36.193 --> 00:04:38.756
"Liar!" screamed Macbeth.

50
00:04:38.756 --> 00:04:45.289
"I swear. Less than a mile from the castle, you can see it coming—a moving forest!"

51
00:04:45.289 --> 00:04:55.344
"If you speak falsely," said Macbeth, "I'll hang you from a tree until you starve to death! Sound the alarm! To battle! To battle!"

52
00:04:55.346 --> 00:05:00.654
As bells rang throughout Dunsinane Castle, trumpets rang in the field outside.

53
00:05:00.656 --> 00:05:05.283
The attacking soldiers dropped their camouflage of tree branches and charged.

54
00:05:05.284 --> 00:05:09.581
The battle was grisly, but Macbeth's soldiers fought without heart.

55
00:05:09.583 --> 00:05:15.265
Malcolm's soldiers, on the other hand, had the courage of hope and battled their way inside the castle.

56
00:05:15.267 --> 00:05:19.472
In the main courtyard, ferocious Macbeth fought on, undefeated.

57
00:05:19.474 --> 00:05:22.568
Fallen soldiers lay on the ground all around him.

58
00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:27.609
Siward, seeing him, called out, "There's the tyrant!" and led a charge.

59
00:05:27.611 --> 00:05:31.491
Their swords clashed briefly before Macbeth struck him down.

60
00:05:31.493 --> 00:05:38.960
"Here lies a man born of a woman!" laughed Macbeth, pulling his sword from Siward's side.

61
00:05:38.962 --> 00:05:43.358
From behind him, a voice yelled, "Turn, you hellhound, turn!"

62
00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:52.106
Macbeth spun around to face Macduff. "You should avoid me, Macduff. I have enough of your family's blood on my hands."

63
00:05:52.108 --> 00:06:00.163
"I have no words for you," answered Macduff, swinging at his adversary. "My voice is in my sword!"

64
00:06:00.165 --> 00:06:06.210
The clang of their weapons rang loudly above the battle as Macbeth struck back, blow for blow.

65
00:06:06.212 --> 00:06:10.973
"You can make me bleed as easily as you can hurt the air," he said.

66
00:06:10.975 --> 00:06:16.514
"I lead a charmed life; I cannot fall to any man of woman born!"

67
00:06:16.516 --> 00:06:22.421
Macduff forced Macbeth's sword aside. "Then your charm has abandoned you!" he said.

68
00:06:22.423 --> 00:06:26.919
"My mother died as I was born, and a surgeon cut me from her womb!"

69
00:06:26.921 --> 00:06:32.833
Macbeth's face went white. "The witches have tricked me by juggling words!"

70
00:06:32.835 --> 00:06:37.605
"All hope for you is gone," said Macduff. "Surrender now."

71
00:06:37.607 --> 00:06:42.943
"I will not yield! Nor kiss the ground before Malcolm's feet!

72
00:06:42.944 --> 00:06:50.965
Though Birnam Wood has come to Dunsinane, though a man not born of a woman opposes me, I will fight to my last breath."

73
00:06:50.965 --> 00:06:53.864
Macbeth gripped his sword with both hands.

74
00:06:53.866 --> 00:06:59.932
"Lay on, Macduff, and damned be the first of us to cry for mercy!"

75
00:06:59.933 --> 00:07:07.646
Macduff lunged at Macbeth with a cry, and the two fought on among the struggling men and flashing weapons in combat around them.

76
00:07:07.647 --> 00:07:13.535
Malcolm entered the castle with Ross, accompanied by guards. The battle was going in their favor.

77
00:07:13.536 --> 00:07:19.170
In fact, many of Macbeth's soldiers had abandoned him and turned to fight with Malcolm's men.

78
00:07:19.171 --> 00:07:23.329
"It looks as though our victory is cheaply bought," said Malcolm.

79
00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:25.518
"Where are Siward and Macduff?"

80
00:07:25.518 --> 00:07:29.658
"Macduff is missing, and Siward is slain," said Ross.

81
00:07:29.658 --> 00:07:34.766
"God be with him," Malcolm said. Just then, Macduff approached.

82
00:07:34.767 --> 00:07:38.259
Held high on the point of his sword was Macbeth's head.

83
00:07:38.261 --> 00:07:44.727
"Behold the tyrant's cursed head!" cried Macduff. "Scotland is free!"

84
00:07:44.729 --> 00:07:50.108
And all around, the injured and weary soldiers put down their weapons and cheered.

85
00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:57.234
Malcolm quietly bowed his head. It would take much work to undo the tragedy Macbeth had brought to his land.

